1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,291
Third normal form starts from second, then hunts a longer chain.

2
00:00:04,291 --> 00:00:09,138
A non-key column must depend on the key, not on another non-key column.

3
00:00:09,138 --> 00:00:13,375
customers has customer_id, city, state.

4
00:00:13,375 --> 00:00:16,972
state depends on city more than it depends on the customer.

5
00:00:16,972 --> 00:00:18,583
Maya lives in Austin.

6
00:00:18,583 --> 00:00:20,333
Austin is in Texas.

7
00:00:20,333 --> 00:00:24,069
If Jordan also lives in Austin, Texas repeats.

8
00:00:24,069 --> 00:00:30,708
If Austin is recoded into a different state in one row, the table disagrees with itself.

9
00:00:30,708 --> 00:00:33,291
That chain is a transitive dependence.

10
00:00:33,291 --> 00:00:37,847
customer_id determines city, city determines state.

11
00:00:37,847 --> 00:00:43,041
So customer_id determines state, but only by walking through city.

12
00:00:43,041 --> 00:00:44,180
Break the chain.

13
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:48,055
cities holds city_id, name, state.

14
00:00:48,055 --> 00:00:50,583
customers holds city_id.

15
00:00:50,583 --> 00:00:52,680
Texas lives in one place.

16
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:57,652
ZIP codes, country codes, tax rates on a region — same shape.

17
00:00:57,652 --> 00:01:01,250
If a fact is about the region, give the region a table.

18
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:03,347
The test is again a sentence.

19
00:01:03,347 --> 00:01:10,291
If I know this non-key value, do I already know that other non-key value, no matter which customer I picked?

20
00:01:10,291 --> 00:01:13,250
Then the second value was not about the customer.

21
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:14,986
Do not over-split trivia.

22
00:01:14,986 --> 00:01:22,319
first_name and last_name both depend on the customer, not on each other in the table sense.

23
00:01:22,319 --> 00:01:24,583
You do not need a names table for that.

24
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:28,777
The dependence that matters is a real-world rule you would enforce.

25
00:01:28,777 --> 00:01:31,625
Austin is always in Texas in your data.

26
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:32,791
That is a rule.

27
00:01:32,791 --> 00:01:36,208
Maya's last name does not determine her first name.

28
00:01:36,208 --> 00:01:40,527
Third normal form is often enough for operational tables.

29
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:43,944
It is the usual stopping point before Boyce–Codd.

30
00:01:43,944 --> 00:01:45,902
You still chose keys first.

31
00:01:45,902 --> 00:01:49,527
Without a clear key, talking about dependence is fog.

32
00:01:49,527 --> 00:01:52,444
Sketch the arrows from key to facts.

33
00:01:52,444 --> 00:01:58,694
If an arrow lands on a fact and another arrow leaves that fact toward a third, you have work.

34
00:01:58,694 --> 00:02:01,125
Move the third fact with the second.

35
00:02:01,125 --> 00:02:03,458
Leave a key behind on the customer.

36
00:02:03,458 --> 00:02:07,041
Transitive chains like to hide in address blocks.

37
00:02:07,041 --> 00:02:11,805
city, state, country, region, tax_zone.

38
00:02:11,805 --> 00:02:14,305
Each step may determine the next.

39
00:02:14,305 --> 00:02:16,861
You do not need five tables on day one.

40
00:02:16,861 --> 00:02:21,958
You do need to notice when two customers in the same city disagree on the state.

41
00:02:21,958 --> 00:02:25,027
That disagreement is the chain telling on you.

42
00:02:25,027 --> 00:02:28,263
Lookups of codes are third-normal-form friends.

43
00:02:28,263 --> 00:02:35,180
status_id pointing at statuses, instead of a color name copied onto every order.

44
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:37,652
The color is a fact about the status.

45
00:02:37,652 --> 00:02:40,125
Derived totals can look transitive.

46
00:02:40,125 --> 00:02:44,805
line_total plus tax equals invoice_total.

47
00:02:44,805 --> 00:02:47,680
If you store all three, they can disagree.

48
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:53,736
Prefer storing the inputs, compute the output, unless you freeze a snapshot for history.

49
00:02:53,736 --> 00:02:55,472
Again, say which.

50
00:02:55,472 --> 00:02:58,125
Third normal form will not compute for you.

51
00:02:58,125 --> 00:03:02,694
It will stop you storing a computed fact as if it were independent.

52
00:03:02,694 --> 00:03:07,416
When a column is a code plus a name, the name belongs with the code.

53
00:03:07,416 --> 00:03:14,013
country_code and country_name on the customer is the city-state bug in a passport.

54
00:03:14,013 --> 00:03:18,111
countries table, then country_code on the customer.

55
00:03:18,111 --> 00:03:19,875
The hop is the same hop.

56
00:03:19,875 --> 00:03:25,069
You are done when every non-key fact answers to the key and only the key.

57
00:03:25,069 --> 00:03:27,166
No tour through another fact.

58
00:03:27,166 --> 00:03:28,736
That is the whole standard.

59
00:03:28,736 --> 00:03:31,958
Most clean stores stop here and get back to work.

60
00:03:31,958 --> 00:03:37,902
If two rows share a city and fight about the state, you stored a region fact on a person.

61
00:03:37,902 --> 00:03:43,555
Move the fight to a cities table, where it becomes one row and one truth.

62
00:03:43,555 --> 00:03:47,694
That is the entire emotional content of third normal form.

63
00:03:47,694 --> 00:03:49,277
The rest is arrows.

64
00:03:49,277 --> 00:03:51,680
Key to city, city to state.

65
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,222
Cut the second arrow out of customers.

66
00:03:54,222 --> 00:03:56,236
Leave city_id.

67
00:03:56,236 --> 00:03:59,069
Do not leave state behind as a souvenir.

68
00:03:59,069 --> 00:04:00,694
Souvenirs disagree.

69
00:04:00,694 --> 00:04:03,069
Lookup tables are not bureaucracy.

70
00:04:03,069 --> 00:04:06,361
They are how a state stays still while customers move.

71
00:04:06,361 --> 00:04:10,263
When the non-key facts all answer to the key, stop.

72
00:04:10,263 --> 00:04:11,263
Go query.

73
00:04:11,263 --> 00:04:17,444
You update a major name on one student row and forget the other three who share that major.

74
00:04:17,444 --> 00:04:19,041
Reports now disagree.

75
00:04:19,041 --> 00:04:33,569
That is a transitive dependency: student_id determines major_id, major_id determines major_name, so student_id determines major_name through a middle column.

76
00:04:33,569 --> 00:04:40,513
Third normal form, simplified: no non-key column is allowed to depend on another non-key column.

77
00:04:40,513 --> 00:04:43,486
Non-key means not part of the primary key.

78
00:04:43,486 --> 00:04:47,680
You must already be in second, which already required first.

79
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:49,569
The nested dolls still apply.

80
00:04:49,682 --> 00:05:03,007
If department_name lives on employees because department_id is also on employees, department_name depends on department_id, not on employee_id directly.

81
00:05:03,007 --> 00:05:06,020
Move department_name to departments.

82
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:10,076
Employees keep department_id as a foreign key.

83
00:05:10,076 --> 00:05:12,215
The transitive chain is cut.

84
00:05:12,215 --> 00:05:16,715
The determinant of the moved fact becomes the key of the new table.

85
00:05:16,715 --> 00:05:19,770
Columns that really describe the employee stay.

86
00:05:19,770 --> 00:05:26,979
The shared department_id stays in both: as key in departments, as foreign key in employees.

87
00:05:26,979 --> 00:05:29,423
That copy of the key is allowed.

88
00:05:29,423 --> 00:05:31,798
That is not redundancy of a fact.

89
00:05:31,798 --> 00:05:33,118
It is the pointer.

90
00:05:33,118 --> 00:05:35,604
Redundancy is copying the name.

91
00:05:35,604 --> 00:05:40,923
Codd published the relational model and the early normal forms in 1970.

92
00:05:40,923 --> 00:05:44,895
Third was the practical target for most operational schemas.

93
00:05:44,895 --> 00:05:47,104
It stops the major-name problem.

94
00:05:47,104 --> 00:05:50,187
It does not stop every exotic key overlap.

95
00:05:50,187 --> 00:05:53,187
That leftover is Boyce–Codd's story.

96
00:05:53,187 --> 00:05:56,965
For now, hunt arrows from non-key to non-key.

97
00:05:56,965 --> 00:06:08,284
If city_name depends on zip, and zip is not the key of the customer table, zip and city belong in a location table, or zip determines city in a lookup.

98
00:06:08,284 --> 00:06:16,048
If the dependence is not always true — zip that straddles cities — do not encode a lie as third normal form.

99
00:06:16,048 --> 00:06:19,882
Dependencies are about what the business says must be true.

100
00:06:19,882 --> 00:06:23,229
Not about what a sample of twenty rows happened to show.

101
00:06:23,229 --> 00:06:28,451
Ask: if I change this non-key, must that other non-key change with it everywhere?

102
00:06:28,451 --> 00:06:31,604
If yes, they are one fact in two seats.

103
00:06:31,604 --> 00:06:33,548
Give the fact its own table.

104
00:06:33,548 --> 00:06:35,548
Leave the foreign key behind.

105
00:06:35,548 --> 00:06:42,382
Then a student can change major by changing an id, and the name updates because there is only one name row.

106
00:06:42,382 --> 00:06:49,409
Zip codes: if zip determines city in your country, city on customers is transitive through zip.

107
00:06:49,409 --> 00:06:55,284
If it does not, because one zip spans cities, do not split on a false dependency.

108
00:06:55,284 --> 00:06:58,395
Third is only as true as the dependency.

109
00:06:58,395 --> 00:07:02,382
Invented dependencies are how people over-normalize.

110
00:07:02,382 --> 00:07:05,632
Missed dependencies are how major names drift.

111
00:07:05,632 --> 00:07:10,548
Write the arrow, test it with an update story, then split or stay.

112
00:07:10,548 --> 00:07:14,215
That is using third in design, not reciting it.

113
00:07:14,215 --> 00:07:21,284
After the split, JOIN is how reports look the same as the old wide table, with one name to edit.

114
00:07:21,284 --> 00:07:30,465
If the report is slow, that is indexes or denormalization later, not a reason to put major_name back on students as truth.

115
00:07:30,465 --> 00:07:33,020
A cache copy would be labeled a cache.

116
00:07:33,020 --> 00:07:34,798
Truth stays in majors.

117
00:07:34,798 --> 00:07:38,437
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

118
00:07:38,437 --> 00:07:41,493
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

119
00:07:41,493 --> 00:07:43,590
What is a transitive dependency?

120
00:07:43,590 --> 00:07:50,173
Key determines A, A determines B, so B lives on the row through a bridge that is not a key.

121
00:07:50,173 --> 00:07:51,618
Simplified third?

122
00:07:51,618 --> 00:07:55,187
No non-key column depends on another non-key column.

123
00:07:55,187 --> 00:07:57,451
major_name on students?

124
00:07:57,451 --> 00:08:02,104
Move to majors; keep major_id as a foreign key.

125
00:08:02,104 --> 00:08:04,187
Is the foreign key redundancy?

126
00:08:04,187 --> 00:08:04,951
No.

127
00:08:04,951 --> 00:08:06,270
It is the pointer.

128
00:08:06,270 --> 00:08:08,090
The name was the redundancy.

129
00:08:08,090 --> 00:08:10,048
Must first and second hold?

130
00:08:10,048 --> 00:08:10,951
Yes.

131
00:08:10,951 --> 00:08:12,090
Zip and city?

132
00:08:12,090 --> 00:08:16,118
Split only if zip actually determines city in your rules.

133
00:08:16,118 --> 00:08:17,548
False dependency?

134
00:08:17,548 --> 00:08:19,034
Over-normalization.

135
00:08:19,034 --> 00:08:20,618
Do not split on a lie.

136
00:08:20,618 --> 00:08:22,382
Why do reports still join?

137
00:08:22,382 --> 00:08:24,562
Because the name lives next door.

138
00:08:24,562 --> 00:08:25,923
That is success.

139
00:08:25,923 --> 00:08:27,534
If the join is slow?

140
00:08:27,534 --> 00:08:32,840
Indexes or a labeled cache later, not putting truth back on students.

141
00:08:32,840 --> 00:08:34,965
Who published the early forms?

142
00:08:34,965 --> 00:08:37,354
Codd, around 1970.

143
00:08:37,354 --> 00:08:39,048
What does third not catch?

144
00:08:39,048 --> 00:08:43,368
Some overlapping candidate-key cases; that is Boyce–Codd.

145
00:08:43,368 --> 00:08:46,701
advisor_office through advisor_name?

146
00:08:46,701 --> 00:08:48,923
Move offices with advisors.

147
00:08:48,923 --> 00:08:50,132
How do you test?

148
00:08:50,132 --> 00:08:54,854
Change the name once and see whether four rows would have needed four edits.

149
00:08:54,854 --> 00:08:56,409
What question to ask?

150
00:08:56,409 --> 00:09:00,687
If I change this non-key, must that other non-key change everywhere?

151
00:09:00,687 --> 00:09:01,701
If yes?

152
00:09:01,701 --> 00:09:03,618
One fact in two seats.

153
00:09:03,618 --> 00:09:04,493
Split.

154
00:09:04,493 --> 00:09:05,590
Nested order?

155
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:09,909
Atomic cells, whole key, then no non-key chains.

156
00:09:09,909 --> 00:09:11,882
Is JOIN a failure of third?

157
00:09:11,882 --> 00:09:12,645
No.

158
00:09:12,645 --> 00:09:13,770
Cache copy?

159
00:09:13,770 --> 00:09:15,173
Label it a cache.

160
00:09:15,173 --> 00:09:17,312
Truth stays in the parent table.

161
00:09:17,312 --> 00:09:19,173
Sample of twenty rows?

162
00:09:19,173 --> 00:09:21,409
Not enough to invent a dependency.

163
00:09:21,409 --> 00:09:22,743
When is third done?

164
00:09:22,743 --> 00:09:25,812
No non-key to non-key arrows remain.

165
00:09:25,812 --> 00:09:30,534
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

